 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Division by Zero:** When the target point coincides with a probe center, $\tilde{t} = 0$. The formula for $t$ involves division by $\tilde{t}+1$, which is safe, but the spherical coordinate calculation $\arccos(z/\tilde{t})$ requires handling $\tilde{t}=0$ explicitly to avoid NaNs [S1].
-*   **Precision:** Linear interpolation on low-resolution core factors (starting at 64) may introduce aliasing if not upscaled properly. The method uses progressive upscaling to mitigate this [S1].
+*   **Division by Zero:** When the target point coincides with a probe center, $\tilde{t} = 0$ [G]. The formula for $t$ involves division by $\tilde{t}+1$, which is safe, but the spherical coordinate calculation $\arccos(z/\tilde{t})$ requires handling $\tilde{t}=0$ explicitly to avoid NaNs [G].
+*   **Precision:** Linear interpolation on low-resolution core factors (starting at 64) may introduce aliasing if not upscaled properly [S1]. The method uses progressive upscaling to mitigate this [S1].
